    "created_at": "2017-02-15T17:28:01Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-02-15T17:28:01Z",
    "body": "Just for context, the HTML elements Gecko does _not_ apply display:contents to (and this is just what fell out of implementation; it's not a carefully reasoned about list; I'm not suggesting this be specced!) are:\r\n\r\n* `<legend>` when properly in a `<fieldset>` and not absolutely/fixed positioned and not floated.  In other words, the cases when it actually renders as a legend.\r\n* `<img>` when not showing fallback content.\r\n* `<input type=\"image\">` when not showing fallback content.\r\n* `<br>`\r\n* `<wbr>`\r\n* `<textarea>`\r\n* `<select>`\r\n* `<object>` when not showing fallback.\r\n* `<embed>` when not showing fallback (we don't seem to treat `<embed>` always as a replaced element, which doesn't match the spec afaict).\r\n* `<applet>` when not showing fallback.\r\n* `<fieldset>`\r\n* `<frameset>`\r\n* `<frame>`, effectively, but only when inside `<frameset>`.\r\n* `<irame>`\r\n* `<button>`\r\n* `<canvas>` when not showing its fallback content (which it does when script is disabled for the document).\r\n* `<video>`\r\n* `<audio>`\r\n* `<progress>`\r\n* `<meter>`\r\n* `<details>`\r\n\r\nA bunch of these are covered by the \"replaced element\" bit (e.g. the `<canvas>` and `<object>` behavior), of course.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
