    "body": "@bzbarsky The short answer is, we really need a detailed spec for rendering form controls, including interaction with CSS. The long answer...\r\n\r\n> How is <legend>'s content supposed to be rendered when the <legend> itself is display:contents? Does it just go inside the fieldset as if it were DOM kids of the fieldset, or something else? What spec defines this?\r\n\r\nI think that would be a reasonable interpretation. Basically, treat it just like a `legend` that isn't the first `legend` (and hence doesn't trigger the special behavior).\r\n\r\n> Should `<input type=\"image\">` have the same behavior as `<img>` (imo yes, but one of them is submittable and one is not!) and what behavior should that be?\r\n\r\nIt is a replaced element, an empty element, and a form control, so it doesn't actually matter how you categorize it: it'll be treated as `display: none` in either case.\r\n\r\n> `<object>` is submittable. Should display:contents never work on it? Including when it falls back? This part just seems an outright bug in the new spec text.\r\n\r\nI'll raise the case of element-that-should-be-replaced-but-is-falling-back to the CSSWG.\r\n\r\n> If a `<details>` is made `display:contents`, what happens to its `<summary>`? What does clicking on that `<summary>` element do, if anything? What spec defines this?\r\n\r\nThe HTML spec defines `details` as a (non-replaced) block box; removing a block box from the rendering tree is well-defined. `display: contents` explicitly has no effect on event handling, so there's no reason to expect that  the behavior of `summary` would be affected, and neither its rendering nor that of the rest of `details`'s contents are tied into `details`'s principal box, so there's no ambiguity there afaict.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
