    "created_at": "2017-03-09T17:37:03Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-03-09T17:37:03Z",
    "body": "> It is a replaced element, an empty element, and a form control\r\n\r\nIt's not a replaced element when the image fails to load and it shows the alt text instead.\r\n\r\nAn `<img>` is not a form control.  Both are \"empty elements\", but that's a parser-specific concept; once you've got a DOM you shouldn't have to worry about it, ideally.  I'm not very happy with using that concept on the rendering level, where it was never used before...\r\n\r\n> The HTML spec defines details as a (non-replaced) block box;\r\n\r\nSort of.  It has some weird behavior in terms of hiding its non-summary kids.  This is defined in terms of a vague \"shadow tree\" concept that doesn't actually exist yet, unfortunately, so in practice at least some implementations end up using a special box type for details that supports hiding the kids.\r\n\r\nI also seem to recall incompatibilities between the way HTML specs this and how sites actually expect it to work.  :(\r\n\r\nThis isn't just your issue to solve, obviously, but it's something that needs to get sorted out.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
